{"subjectId":"dsa-basics","id":"singly_linked","title":"Linked List","group":"Linked Lists","order":11,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A linked list is a linear collection whose elements are <strong>not</strong> stored next to each other in memory: every node holds a value and a pointer to the next node. Insertion and deletion at a known position are O(1), at the price of O(n) access by index.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Singly linked list</strong> — a sequence of <strong>nodes</strong>, each containing a <code>data</code> field and a <code>next</code> reference to the following node. The first node is the <strong>head</strong>; the list ends at the node whose <code>next</code> is <code>None</code>/<code>null</code>. Only the head is stored — everything else is reached by following <code>next</code> pointers.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>A singly linked list with three nodes — only the head reference is stored by the program</figcaption><pre><code>head\n ↓\n┌──────┬──────┐    ┌──────┬──────┐    ┌──────┬──────┐\n│  10  │  ●───┼───▶│  20  │  ●───┼───▶│  30  │  ∅   │\n└──────┴──────┘    └──────┴──────┘    └──────┴──────┘\n  data   next        data   next        data   next = None (end of list)</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>Structure of a node</h3>\n<p>A node is a tiny object (or struct) with two fields. Nodes are allocated one at a time, so the list grows and shrinks without ever copying or resizing an array. Traversal starts at the head and follows <code>next</code> until it becomes <code>None</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self.data = data\n        self.next = None             # no next node yet\n\ndef show(head):\n    parts = []\n    curr = head\n    while curr is not None:          # traversal: follow next until the end\n        parts.append(str(curr.data))\n        curr = curr.next\n    print(\" -&gt; \".join(parts))\n\ndef length(head):\n    n, curr = 0, head\n    while curr:\n        n += 1\n        curr = curr.next\n    return n\n\nhead = Node(10)                      # build by hand: 10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30\nhead.next = Node(20)\nhead.next.next = Node(30)\nshow(head)\nprint(\"length:\", length(head))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int data;\n    Node* next;\n    Node(int d) : data(d), next(nullptr) {}   // no next node yet\n};\n\nvoid show(Node* head) {\n    for (Node* curr = head; curr; curr = curr-&gt;next) {   // traversal: follow next until null\n        cout &lt;&lt; curr-&gt;data;\n        if (curr-&gt;next) cout &lt;&lt; \" -&gt; \";\n    }\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}\n\nint length(Node* head) {\n    int n = 0;\n    for (Node* curr = head; curr; curr = curr-&gt;next) n++;\n    return n;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Node* head = new Node(10);           // build by hand: 10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30\n    head-&gt;next = new Node(20);\n    head-&gt;next-&gt;next = new Node(30);\n    show(head);\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"length: \" &lt;&lt; length(head) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class Node {\n        int data; Node next;              // next stays null until linked\n        Node(int d) { data = d; }\n    }\n\n    static void show(Node head) {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (Node curr = head; curr != null; curr = curr.next) {   // traversal\n            sb.append(curr.data);\n            if (curr.next != null) sb.append(\" -&gt; \");\n        }\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n\n    static int length(Node head) {\n        int n = 0;\n        for (Node curr = head; curr != null; curr = curr.next) n++;\n        return n;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Node head = new Node(10);         // build by hand: 10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30\n        head.next = new Node(20);\n        head.next.next = new Node(30);\n        show(head);\n        System.out.println(\"length: \" + length(head));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Node {\n    constructor(data) { this.data = data; this.next = null; }   // no next node yet\n}\n\nfunction show(head) {\n    const parts = [];\n    for (let curr = head; curr !== null; curr = curr.next)     // traversal\n        parts.push(curr.data);\n    console.log(parts.join(\" -&gt; \"));\n}\n\nfunction length(head) {\n    let n = 0;\n    for (let curr = head; curr !== null; curr = curr.next) n++;\n    return n;\n}\n\nconst head = new Node(10);               // build by hand: 10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30\nhead.next = new Node(20);\nhead.next.next = new Node(30);\nshow(head);\nconsole.log(\"length:\", length(head));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30\nlength: 3</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n) traversal</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n) for n nodes</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>The list has no <code>size</code> unless you maintain a counter: <code>length</code> walks all nodes in O(n). Real implementations wrap the head in a <code>LinkedList</code> class that also stores <code>size</code> (and often a <code>tail</code>).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-scale\"></i>Why — and why not — a linked list</h3>\n<div class=\"grid grid--3\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-maximize\"></i>Dynamic size</h3><p>Grows one node at a time; no capacity to guess, no O(n) resize copy.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>O(1) insert / delete at a known node</h3><p>Re-link two pointers. An array must shift every later element.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-grid-dots\"></i>No contiguous block needed</h3><p>Nodes can live anywhere in memory — useful when large arrays cannot be allocated.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search-off\"></i>No random access</h3><p><code>list[i]</code> means walking <code>i</code> nodes: O(n). Binary search is impossible.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-database\"></i>Extra memory</h3><p>One pointer per element (8 bytes on 64-bit) — a list of small ints doubles its footprint.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-cpu\"></i>Cache unfriendly</h3><p>Nodes are scattered; each <code>next</code> is a potential cache miss, so traversals are slower than over an array of the same size.</p></div></div>\n<p>Linked lists are the building block of other structures: stacks and queues (O(1) at the ends), hash-table chains, adjacency lists in graphs, and the LRU cache (a doubly linked list plus a hash map).</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-check\"></i>Operations and their cost</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Time</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Insert at beginning</td><td>O(1)</td><td>New node points at the old head; the head reference moves. No traversal.</td></tr><tr><td>Insert at end</td><td>O(n) — O(1) with a tail pointer</td><td>Without a tail you must walk to the last node first.</td></tr><tr><td>Delete head</td><td>O(1)</td><td>Head moves to <code>head.next</code>.</td></tr><tr><td>Delete last</td><td>O(n)</td><td>Need the <em>second-to-last</em> node to clear its <code>next</code>; a singly linked list cannot go backwards.</td></tr><tr><td>Insert / delete after a given node</td><td>O(1)</td><td>Only pointers of the given node and its neighbour change.</td></tr><tr><td>Search</td><td>O(n)</td><td>Sequential scan; nothing better is possible without random access.</td></tr><tr><td>Access by index</td><td>O(n)</td><td>Walk <code>i</code> links.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-bar-to-left\"></i>Insert at the beginning</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>list = 20 -&gt; 30, insert 10 at the beginning</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Create the node, make it point at the current head, and return it as the new head. No node is visited, so it is O(1) regardless of the list length — the one place where a linked list beats an array outright.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Insert at the beginning: two pointer assignments</figcaption><pre><code>before:   head ──▶ [20] ──▶ [30] ──▶ ∅\n\nstep 1:   new = [10]           new.next = head        [10] ──▶ [20] ──▶ [30] ──▶ ∅\nstep 2:   head = new                                   head ──▶ [10] ──▶ [20] ──▶ [30] ──▶ ∅</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self.data = data\n        self.next = None\n\ndef insert_at_beginning(head, data):\n    new_node = Node(data)\n    new_node.next = head         # new node points at the old head\n    return new_node              # it is the new head — caller must store it\n\ndef show(head):\n    parts = []\n    while head:\n        parts.append(str(head.data))\n        head = head.next\n    print(\" -&gt; \".join(parts))\n\nhead = None\nfor x in (30, 20, 10):           # insert in reverse to get 10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30\n    head = insert_at_beginning(head, x)\n    show(head)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int data; Node* next;\n    Node(int d) : data(d), next(nullptr) {}\n};\n\nNode* insertAtBeginning(Node* head, int data) {\n    Node* newNode = new Node(data);\n    newNode-&gt;next = head;        // new node points at the old head\n    return newNode;              // it is the new head — caller must store it\n}\n\nvoid show(Node* head) {\n    for (; head; head = head-&gt;next) cout &lt;&lt; head-&gt;data &lt;&lt; (head-&gt;next ? \" -&gt; \" : \"\\n\");\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Node* head = nullptr;\n    for (int x : {30, 20, 10}) {     // insert in reverse to get 10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30\n        head = insertAtBeginning(head, x);\n        show(head);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class Node {\n        int data; Node next;\n        Node(int d) { data = d; }\n    }\n\n    static Node insertAtBeginning(Node head, int data) {\n        Node newNode = new Node(data);\n        newNode.next = head;         // new node points at the old head\n        return newNode;              // it is the new head — caller must store it\n    }\n\n    static void show(Node head) {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (; head != null; head = head.next) sb.append(head.data).append(head.next != null ? \" -&gt; \" : \"\");\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Node head = null;\n        for (int x : new int[]{30, 20, 10}) {   // insert in reverse to get 10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30\n            head = insertAtBeginning(head, x);\n            show(head);\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Node {\n    constructor(data) { this.data = data; this.next = null; }\n}\n\nfunction insertAtBeginning(head, data) {\n    const newNode = new Node(data);\n    newNode.next = head;             // new node points at the old head\n    return newNode;                  // it is the new head — caller must store it\n}\n\nfunction show(head) {\n    const parts = [];\n    for (; head !== null; head = head.next) parts.push(head.data);\n    console.log(parts.join(\" -&gt; \"));\n}\n\nlet head = null;\nfor (const x of [30, 20, 10]) {      // insert in reverse to get 10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30\n    head = insertAtBeginning(head, x);\n    show(head);\n}</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>30\n20 -&gt; 30\n10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(1)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>Functions that change the head must <strong>return the new head</strong> (or work on a wrapper object). Writing <code>insert_at_beginning(head, 10)</code> without <code>head = …</code> leaves the caller pointing at the old second node — the new node is lost.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-bar-to-right\"></i>Insert at the end</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>list = 10 -&gt; 20, insert 30 at the end</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>list = empty, insert 10</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>10</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Walk to the last node (the one whose <code>next</code> is <code>None</code>) and hang the new node there. The walk makes it O(n); keeping a <code>tail</code> pointer in a wrapper class makes it O(1). The empty list is a special case: the new node simply becomes the head.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self.data = data\n        self.next = None\n\ndef insert_at_end(head, data):\n    new_node = Node(data)\n    if head is None:                 # empty list: the new node is the head\n        return new_node\n    current = head\n    while current.next is not None:  # O(n) walk to the last node\n        current = current.next\n    current.next = new_node\n    return head                      # head is unchanged\n\ndef show(head):\n    parts = []\n    while head:\n        parts.append(str(head.data))\n        head = head.next\n    print(\" -&gt; \".join(parts))\n\nhead = None\nfor x in (10, 20, 30):\n    head = insert_at_end(head, x)\n    show(head)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int data; Node* next;\n    Node(int d) : data(d), next(nullptr) {}\n};\n\nNode* insertAtEnd(Node* head, int data) {\n    Node* newNode = new Node(data);\n    if (!head) return newNode;           // empty list: the new node is the head\n    Node* current = head;\n    while (current-&gt;next) current = current-&gt;next;   // O(n) walk to the last node\n    current-&gt;next = newNode;\n    return head;                         // head is unchanged\n}\n\nvoid show(Node* head) {\n    for (; head; head = head-&gt;next) cout &lt;&lt; head-&gt;data &lt;&lt; (head-&gt;next ? \" -&gt; \" : \"\\n\");\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Node* head = nullptr;\n    for (int x : {10, 20, 30}) {\n        head = insertAtEnd(head, x);\n        show(head);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class Node {\n        int data; Node next;\n        Node(int d) { data = d; }\n    }\n\n    static Node insertAtEnd(Node head, int data) {\n        Node newNode = new Node(data);\n        if (head == null) return newNode;    // empty list: the new node is the head\n        Node current = head;\n        while (current.next != null) current = current.next;   // O(n) walk to the last node\n        current.next = newNode;\n        return head;                         // head is unchanged\n    }\n\n    static void show(Node head) {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (; head != null; head = head.next) sb.append(head.data).append(head.next != null ? \" -&gt; \" : \"\");\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Node head = null;\n        for (int x : new int[]{10, 20, 30}) {\n            head = insertAtEnd(head, x);\n            show(head);\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Node {\n    constructor(data) { this.data = data; this.next = null; }\n}\n\nfunction insertAtEnd(head, data) {\n    const newNode = new Node(data);\n    if (head === null) return newNode;       // empty list: the new node is the head\n    let current = head;\n    while (current.next !== null) current = current.next;   // O(n) walk to the last node\n    current.next = newNode;\n    return head;                             // head is unchanged\n}\n\nfunction show(head) {\n    const parts = [];\n    for (; head !== null; head = head.next) parts.push(head.data);\n    console.log(parts.join(\" -&gt; \"));\n}\n\nlet head = null;\nfor (const x of [10, 20, 30]) {\n    head = insertAtEnd(head, x);\n    show(head);\n}</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10\n10 -&gt; 20\n10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n) — O(1) with a tail pointer</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Building a list of <code>n</code> elements by repeated <code>insert_at_end</code> costs O(n²) without a tail pointer. Either keep a tail, or insert at the beginning and reverse once at the end.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-bar-left\"></i>Delete the head</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>list = 10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>20 -&gt; 30</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>list = 10</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>empty</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>The second node becomes the head: return <code>head.next</code>. In C++ free the old node; in Python, Java and JavaScript the garbage collector reclaims it once nothing refers to it. An empty list stays empty.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self.data = data\n        self.next = None\n\ndef delete_head(head):\n    if head is None:\n        return None                  # nothing to delete\n    return head.next                 # the old head is garbage-collected\n\ndef show(head):\n    parts = []\n    while head:\n        parts.append(str(head.data))\n        head = head.next\n    print(\" -&gt; \".join(parts) if parts else \"empty\")\n\nhead = Node(10); head.next = Node(20); head.next.next = Node(30)\nshow(head)\nfor _ in range(4):\n    head = delete_head(head)\n    print(\"after delete head: \", end=\"\")\n    show(head)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int data; Node* next;\n    Node(int d) : data(d), next(nullptr) {}\n};\n\nNode* deleteHead(Node* head) {\n    if (!head) return nullptr;           // nothing to delete\n    Node* next = head-&gt;next;\n    delete head;                         // free the old head\n    return next;\n}\n\nvoid show(Node* head) {\n    if (!head) { cout &lt;&lt; \"empty\\n\"; return; }\n    for (; head; head = head-&gt;next) cout &lt;&lt; head-&gt;data &lt;&lt; (head-&gt;next ? \" -&gt; \" : \"\\n\");\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Node* head = new Node(10); head-&gt;next = new Node(20); head-&gt;next-&gt;next = new Node(30);\n    show(head);\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) {\n        head = deleteHead(head);\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"after delete head: \";\n        show(head);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class Node {\n        int data; Node next;\n        Node(int d) { data = d; }\n    }\n\n    static Node deleteHead(Node head) {\n        if (head == null) return null;       // nothing to delete\n        return head.next;                    // the old head is garbage-collected\n    }\n\n    static void show(Node head) {\n        if (head == null) { System.out.println(\"empty\"); return; }\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (; head != null; head = head.next) sb.append(head.data).append(head.next != null ? \" -&gt; \" : \"\");\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Node head = new Node(10); head.next = new Node(20); head.next.next = new Node(30);\n        show(head);\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) {\n            head = deleteHead(head);\n            System.out.print(\"after delete head: \");\n            show(head);\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Node {\n    constructor(data) { this.data = data; this.next = null; }\n}\n\nfunction deleteHead(head) {\n    if (head === null) return null;          // nothing to delete\n    return head.next;                        // the old head is garbage-collected\n}\n\nfunction show(head) {\n    const parts = [];\n    for (; head !== null; head = head.next) parts.push(head.data);\n    console.log(parts.length ? parts.join(\" -&gt; \") : \"empty\");\n}\n\nlet head = new Node(10); head.next = new Node(20); head.next.next = new Node(30);\nshow(head);\nfor (let i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++) {\n    head = deleteHead(head);\n    process.stdout.write(\"after delete head: \");\n    show(head);\n}</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30\nafter delete head: 20 -&gt; 30\nafter delete head: 30\nafter delete head: empty\nafter delete head: empty</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(1)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(1)</span></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-bar-right\"></i>Delete the last node</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>list = 10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>10 -&gt; 20</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>list = 10</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>empty</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>A singly linked list cannot step backwards, so to drop the last node we must stop at the <strong>second-to-last</strong> one (<code>current.next.next is None</code>) and clear its <code>next</code>. That walk is what makes the operation O(n), while deleting the head is O(1).</p></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self.data = data\n        self.next = None\n\ndef delete_last(head):\n    if head is None or head.next is None:\n        return None                          # 0 or 1 node → empty list\n    current = head\n    while current.next.next is not None:     # stop at the second-to-last node\n        current = current.next\n    current.next = None\n    return head\n\ndef show(head):\n    parts = []\n    while head:\n        parts.append(str(head.data))\n        head = head.next\n    print(\" -&gt; \".join(parts) if parts else \"empty\")\n\nhead = Node(10); head.next = Node(20); head.next.next = Node(30)\nshow(head)\nfor _ in range(3):\n    head = delete_last(head)\n    print(\"after delete last: \", end=\"\")\n    show(head)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int data; Node* next;\n    Node(int d) : data(d), next(nullptr) {}\n};\n\nNode* deleteLast(Node* head) {\n    if (!head || !head-&gt;next) { delete head; return nullptr; }   // 0 or 1 node → empty list\n    Node* current = head;\n    while (current-&gt;next-&gt;next) current = current-&gt;next;        // stop at the second-to-last node\n    delete current-&gt;next;\n    current-&gt;next = nullptr;\n    return head;\n}\n\nvoid show(Node* head) {\n    if (!head) { cout &lt;&lt; \"empty\\n\"; return; }\n    for (; head; head = head-&gt;next) cout &lt;&lt; head-&gt;data &lt;&lt; (head-&gt;next ? \" -&gt; \" : \"\\n\");\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Node* head = new Node(10); head-&gt;next = new Node(20); head-&gt;next-&gt;next = new Node(30);\n    show(head);\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {\n        head = deleteLast(head);\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"after delete last: \";\n        show(head);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class Node {\n        int data; Node next;\n        Node(int d) { data = d; }\n    }\n\n    static Node deleteLast(Node head) {\n        if (head == null || head.next == null) return null;       // 0 or 1 node → empty list\n        Node current = head;\n        while (current.next.next != null) current = current.next; // stop at the second-to-last node\n        current.next = null;\n        return head;\n    }\n\n    static void show(Node head) {\n        if (head == null) { System.out.println(\"empty\"); return; }\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (; head != null; head = head.next) sb.append(head.data).append(head.next != null ? \" -&gt; \" : \"\");\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Node head = new Node(10); head.next = new Node(20); head.next.next = new Node(30);\n        show(head);\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {\n            head = deleteLast(head);\n            System.out.print(\"after delete last: \");\n            show(head);\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Node {\n    constructor(data) { this.data = data; this.next = null; }\n}\n\nfunction deleteLast(head) {\n    if (head === null || head.next === null) return null;        // 0 or 1 node → empty list\n    let current = head;\n    while (current.next.next !== null) current = current.next;   // stop at the second-to-last node\n    current.next = null;\n    return head;\n}\n\nfunction show(head) {\n    const parts = [];\n    for (; head !== null; head = head.next) parts.push(head.data);\n    console.log(parts.length ? parts.join(\" -&gt; \") : \"empty\");\n}\n\nlet head = new Node(10); head.next = new Node(20); head.next.next = new Node(30);\nshow(head);\nfor (let i = 0; i &lt; 3; i++) {\n    head = deleteLast(head);\n    process.stdout.write(\"after delete last: \");\n    show(head);\n}</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30\nafter delete last: 10 -&gt; 20\nafter delete last: 10\nafter delete last: empty</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(1)</span></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>Search in a linked list</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>list = 10 -&gt; 20 -&gt; 30, target = 20</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>position 2</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>target = 40</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>-1</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Walk from the head comparing each node's data with the target and counting positions (1-indexed). Return the position on a match, <code>-1</code> after falling off the end. Without random access, a linear scan is the only option — even on a sorted list.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self.data = data\n        self.next = None\n\ndef search_list(head, target):\n    current, position = head, 1\n    while current is not None:\n        if current.data == target:\n            return position\n        current = current.next\n        position += 1\n    return -1                            # fell off the end\n\ndef report(head, x):\n    pos = search_list(head, x)\n    print(f\"search {x}:\", f\"position {pos}\" if pos != -1 else -1)\n\nhead = Node(10); head.next = Node(20); head.next.next = Node(30)\nfor x in (20, 10, 40):\n    report(head, x)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int data; Node* next;\n    Node(int d) : data(d), next(nullptr) {}\n};\n\nint searchList(Node* head, int target) {\n    int position = 1;\n    for (Node* current = head; current; current = current-&gt;next, position++)\n        if (current-&gt;data == target) return position;\n    return -1;                           // fell off the end\n}\n\nvoid report(Node* head, int x) {\n    int pos = searchList(head, x);\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"search \" &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; \": \";\n    if (pos != -1) cout &lt;&lt; \"position \" &lt;&lt; pos &lt;&lt; \"\\n\"; else cout &lt;&lt; \"-1\\n\";\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Node* head = new Node(10); head-&gt;next = new Node(20); head-&gt;next-&gt;next = new Node(30);\n    for (int x : {20, 10, 40}) report(head, x);\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class Node {\n        int data; Node next;\n        Node(int d) { data = d; }\n    }\n\n    static int searchList(Node head, int target) {\n        int position = 1;\n        for (Node current = head; current != null; current = current.next, position++)\n            if (current.data == target) return position;\n        return -1;                           // fell off the end\n    }\n\n    static void report(Node head, int x) {\n        int pos = searchList(head, x);\n        System.out.println(\"search \" + x + \": \" + (pos != -1 ? \"position \" + pos : \"-1\"));\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Node head = new Node(10); head.next = new Node(20); head.next.next = new Node(30);\n        for (int x : new int[]{20, 10, 40}) report(head, x);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Node {\n    constructor(data) { this.data = data; this.next = null; }\n}\n\nfunction searchList(head, target) {\n    let position = 1;\n    for (let current = head; current !== null; current = current.next, position++)\n        if (current.data === target) return position;\n    return -1;                               // fell off the end\n}\n\nfunction report(head, x) {\n    const pos = searchList(head, x);\n    console.log(`search ${x}:`, pos !== -1 ? `position ${pos}` : -1);\n}\n\nconst head = new Node(10); head.next = new Node(20); head.next.next = new Node(30);\nfor (const x of [20, 10, 40]) report(head, x);</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>search 20: position 2\nsearch 10: position 1\nsearch 40: -1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(1)</span></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-scale\"></i>Array vs linked list</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Array / dynamic array (contiguous)</th><th>Linked list (pointers)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Access by index</td><td>O(1) — address = base + i · size</td><td>O(n) — walk <code>i</code> links</td></tr><tr><td>Search (unsorted)</td><td>O(n)</td><td>O(n)</td></tr><tr><td>Search (sorted)</td><td>O(log n) binary search</td><td>O(n) — no random access, binary search impossible</td></tr><tr><td>Insert / delete at start</td><td>O(n) — shift every element</td><td>O(1) — re-link the head</td></tr><tr><td>Insert / delete at end</td><td>O(1) amortised (occasional resize copy)</td><td>O(n), or O(1) with a tail pointer (delete last stays O(n) for singly linked)</td></tr><tr><td>Insert / delete in the middle (position known)</td><td>O(n) — shift the tail</td><td>O(1) — once you hold the previous node</td></tr><tr><td>Extra memory</td><td>None per element; unused capacity in dynamic arrays</td><td>One <code>next</code> pointer per node</td></tr><tr><td>Memory layout</td><td>Contiguous — cache friendly, prefetchable</td><td>Scattered — pointer chasing, cache misses</td></tr><tr><td>Size</td><td>Fixed, or resized by copying</td><td>Grows node by node</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Default to an array / dynamic array: random access and cache locality win most of the time. Reach for a linked list when you need many O(1) insertions or deletions at known nodes (queues, LRU caches, adjacency lists, splicing lists together) or cannot afford resize copies.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-message-question\"></i>Interview Q&amp;A</h3>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is inserting at the front O(1) in a linked list but O(n) in an array?</summary><div><p>An array keeps elements contiguous, so making room at index 0 means moving every existing element one slot right. A linked list has no positions — a new node just points at the old head and becomes the new head; nothing else moves.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is binary search not possible on a linked list?</summary><div><p>Binary search needs the middle element in O(1). In a linked list reaching the middle costs O(n/2) pointer hops, so every probe is O(n) and the search becomes O(n) overall — no better than a linear scan.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you find the middle of a linked list in one pass?</summary><div><p>Two pointers: <code>slow</code> moves one node per step, <code>fast</code> two. When <code>fast</code> reaches the end, <code>slow</code> is at the middle. The same trick (Floyd's algorithm) detects a cycle: if <code>fast</code> ever meets <code>slow</code>, the list loops.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How would you reverse a singly linked list?</summary><div><p>Iterate with three pointers <code>prev</code>, <code>curr</code>, <code>next</code>: save <code>next = curr.next</code>, redirect <code>curr.next = prev</code>, then advance <code>prev = curr</code>, <code>curr = next</code>. When <code>curr</code> is <code>None</code>, <code>prev</code> is the new head. O(n) time, O(1) space.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When is a linked list a better choice than a dynamic array?</summary><div><p>When the workload is dominated by inserts/deletes at the ends or at nodes you already hold (a queue, an LRU cache, merging sorted lists), when element moves are expensive, or when a guaranteed O(1) without occasional resize pauses matters. For index-heavy or cache-sensitive code, arrays win.</p></div></details></section>"}